window's looks, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Its licence travels
with it, in licenses/FlatLaf-LICENSE.txt of each distribution.


Embedded Java runtime
=====================

The self-contained distributions — the Windows and Linux archives — carry a Java runtime
linked (jlink) from the Eclipse Temurin 25 JDK: the modules the program needs, with the
program and FlatLaf among them.

Eclipse Temurin (OpenJDK) is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 2,
with the Classpath Exception (GPLv2 + CPE). The Classpath Exception is what permits
distributing CVEScanner together with this runtime without affecting the licence of
CVEScanner's own code.

The runtime's own licence and notices travel with it, in the jre/legal/ directory of
each distribution:
  - jre/legal/java.base/LICENSE              the GNU General Public License, version 2
  - jre/legal/java.base/ASSEMBLY_EXCEPTION   the Classpath Exception
  - jre/legal/java.base/ADDITIONAL_LICENSE_INFO
  - jre/legal/<module>/*                     the per-component notices of each module
The exact runtime build is recorded in jre/release.

Source code for the OpenJDK runtime is available from:
  - Eclipse Temurin:  https://adoptium.net/temurin/releases/?version=25
  - OpenJDK project:  https://github.com/openjdk/jdk25u


Vulnerability databases
=======================

CVEScanner holds no vulnerability data: its user downloads the databases and gives
their zip files to the program, which reads them where they are. Each comes with its
own terms, which bind whoever downloads and uses it:
  - GitHub Advisory Database (https://github.com/github/advisory-database): Creative
    Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).
  - CVE List (https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5): the CVE Program's terms of use,
    https://www.cve.org/Legal/TermsOfUse.
  - OSV.dev exports (https://osv.dev): each record under the licence of the source it
    comes from, as osv.dev documents.
  - NVD's CVE feeds (https://nvd.nist.gov/feeds/json/cve/2.0/): NVD data, which the NIST
    publishes for public use. This product uses data from the NVD API but is not
    endorsed or certified by the NVD.
The reports CVEScanner writes quote the advisories' identifiers, summaries and links.


Example projects
================

The projects of examples/ are original, written for this project; they fall under the same licence. They name
packages published by others (Apache Log4j, Spring, lodash…) at versions with known vulnerabilities, without holding
any of their code: nothing in them is installed or run.


Sample files
============

The projects and databases of samples/ are original, written for this project by
samples/make_fixtures.py; they fall under the same licence. Their advisories are made
up for the tests, though some borrow the identifiers of real CVEs: they are not
vulnerability data. The samples are not part of the distributions.
